{"text": "covincron 6 euRLING\n\nPRIVILEGED AND CONFIDENTIAL\nATTORNEY-CLIENT COMMUNTCATTON\n\nApril 6, 1992\n\nMEWORANDUY.\n\u2018This memorandum considers whether toxicological data\nobtained from testing of substances under investigation for\nuse, but never actually used, as additives of tobacco could\ngive rise to substantial risk reporting obligations under\nsection 8(e) of the Toxic Substances Control Act (\"TSCA\"), 15\nU.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 2601 et seq. This memorandum supplements the\nJanuary 8, 1992 memorandum from Covington & Burling which\nconcludes that EPA is unlikely to take the position that data\nfrom testing of additives used in tobacco products are subject\nto section 8(e) reporting. As explained in this memorandum,\nwhere RED activities have a clearly defined and documented\nexclusive tobacco purpose, associated test data would not be\nsubject to section 8(e) reporting.\nSection 8(e) reporting requirements apply to persons\nwho engage in certain commercial activities involving\n\u201cchemical substances\" or \"mixtures\" of chemical substances as\ndefined under the Act. Product research and development (R&D)\nis a commercial activity subject to section 8(e) reporting\nrequirements. See Section 8(e) Statement of Interpretation\nand Enforcement Policy, 43 Fed. Reg. 11110, 11111 (1978); id.\nat 11115 (App. B, Response to Comment 31).\nNot all commercial uses of chemicals fall within\n\nTSCA jurisdiction. Section 3(2) of the Act excludes\n\n566602897",
